## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design incremental geometry data structures, reason about dominance, and assess batch-update trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Pareto frontier maintenance
- Batch incremental updates
- Monotone frontier properties (x increasing, y decreasing)
- Trade-offs: update time vs rebuild cost

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: merge batch into frontier
function insertBatch(frontier, batch){
  batch.sort((a,b)=> a.x-b.x);
  // prune intra-batch dominated
  let filtered=[]; let maxY=-Infinity;
  for(let p of batch){ if(p.y>maxY){ filtered.push(p); maxY=p.y; } }
  // merge with frontier (frontier: sorted by x, y strictly decreasing)
  let i=0,j=0; let newFrontier=[];
  while(i<frontier.length || j<filtered.length){
    let a = frontier[i], b = filtered[j];
    if(!a) { newFrontier.push(...filtered.slice(j)); break; }
    if(!b) { newFrontier.push(...frontier.slice(i)); break; }
    if(a.x < b.x){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; }
    else if(b.x < a.x){ newFrontier.push(b); j++; }
    else { // equal x, keep larger y
      if(a.y > b.y){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; j++; }
      else { newFrontier.push(b); i++; j++; }
    }
  }
  // optional prune to maintain y-decreasing property
  return pruneFrontier(newFrontier);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a streaming heavy-hitters approach with bounded memory and clear trade-offs. It focuses on a practical, beginner-friendly variant of the classic Space-Saving problem, tying implementation to batch processing and real-time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Space-Saving / heavy-hitters
- Fixed-memory streaming
- Per-element vs per-batch costs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for Space-Saving with K counters
class Counter { constructor(id, count) { this.id = id; this.count = count; } }
let counters = new Map();
function update(e){
  if (counters.has(e)) { counters.get(e).count++ }
  else {
    if (counters.size < K) { counters.set(e, {count:1}) } 
    else { // replace min
      let minEntry = null; let minCount = Infinity;
      for (let [id, c] of counters) { if (c.count < minCount) { minCount = c.count; minEntry = id; } }
      counters.delete(minEntry);
      counters.set(e, {count: minCount + 1});
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust if the batch size B varies?
- How would you guarantee no false positives beyond a fixed error margin?
